INT. HARBOUR PILOT STATION - OFFICE - NIGHT

A cramped prefab office on stilts above a container yard. Radar sweeps on a small green screen. A kettle, three mugs, a corkboard of tide tables. Rain on a tin roof.

DETECTIVE INSPECTOR RAKESH MALHOTRA (50s, creased raincoat, thorough rather than clever) sits on a plastic chair. His notebook is open on his knee. He has been here for two hours.

Opposite him, harbour pilot ANSELM DRUMMOND (50s, navy jersey, neat grey beard) fills the kettle from a bottle. His hands are slow and exact.

At the desk, HARBOUR CLERK BIRGITTA STRAND (20s, headset, laminated lanyard) taps keys on a battered keyboard. She is crying, but only in the way her throat works.

MALHOTRA
You said you didn't see the container.

DRUMMOND
I said I didn't see it come off the ship.

MALHOTRA
And you didn't see it sit on the quay.

DRUMMOND
It sat on the quay all week. Everyone saw it.

MALHOTRA
Birgitta?

BIRGITTA
(not looking up)
Container 4471. Customs hold on the fourteenth. Released on the sixteenth.

MALHOTRA
Who released it?

BIRGITTA
The system.

MALHOTRA
The system did that on its own?

BIRGITTA
Somebody clicked something.

Drummond sets the kettle on its base and flicks the switch. The element begins to tick. He folds his arms and leans on the counter.

DRUMMOND
Inspector, I've been piloting ships into this harbour for twenty-six years. I bring in tankers the length of the high street through a channel two hundred feet wide. I do not click things.

MALHOTRA
I'm not saying you did.

DRUMMOND
You are saying something.

Malhotra turns a page. The paper rustles. He reads a line to himself, then looks at the corkboard, where the tide table for the fifteenth has a pencil mark in the margin.

MALHOTRA
Whose handwriting is that?

BIRGITTA
(quickly)
Mine.

DRUMMOND
Hers.

MALHOTRA
Neither of you looked.

Birgitta swallows. Drummond rubs his beard with the back of one hand. The kettle starts to roar. Steam beads on the window.

MALHOTRA (CONT'D)
At 2:10 on the fifteenth, a pilot cutter left this station with one man aboard. The log says it went out for a routine inspection of a buoy.

DRUMMOND
It did.

MALHOTRA
The buoy has been missing since March.

Silence. The kettle clicks off. Nobody moves to pour.

BIRGITTA
(very quiet)
It's my fault.

DRUMMOND
Birgitta.

BIRGITTA
I released the hold. He told me it was paperwork. He said the owners would say thank you.

MALHOTRA
Who?

She presses her fingers hard against her mouth. Drummond looks at her for a long time. The ticking of the cooling kettle fills the room. Then he sits, heavily, on the stool beside her, and takes her hand off the keyboard, and holds it in both of his.

DRUMMOND
It was me, Inspector.

MALHOTRA
I thought it might be.

DRUMMOND
She was frightened. I told her what to click.

MALHOTRA
That's what she'll say in court, as well?

DRUMMOND
It's what happened.

Malhotra closes the notebook. Slides it into his coat. Stands, bones popping. He goes to the counter, takes the kettle, and pours three mugs of water over three bare teabags. He sets the first before Birgitta, the second before Drummond, and holds the third himself.

MALHOTRA
Drink it while it's hot. The car's at the gate. It'll wait.

DRUMMOND
Thank you.

MALHOTRA
Don't thank me yet.

Drummond lifts the mug. His hands shake for the first time. A little tea spills onto the tide table, spreading across the pencil mark in a brown stain.

Birgitta lays her head on his shoulder. Outside, a ship's horn sounds, long and low, from beyond the breakwater. It is the sound of something coming home.

FADE OUT.
